<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Open it and you are on today. Write what happened, say how the day was on a scale of 1 to 5 if
    you want to, and save. Saving the same day twice edits the entry you already had, so there is
    never a second one. No tags, no search, no reminders, no streaks: a journal that asks for a few
    words a day, on your own machine.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Every day is a page</h3>
        <p><code>/</code> is today and <code>/2026-09-15</code> is that day; the last thirty days you wrote about are one click away.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>A mood, if you want one</h3>
        <p>1 to 5, or not recorded — the only structured field, kept so your mood can later sit beside everything else a day contained.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Yours to run</h3>
        <p>One container beside Postgres, with sign-in by magic link, migrations at boot, and light or dark per device.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="start">
  <h2>Get started</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <pre class="code"><code>git clone https://github.com/cubicecho/ephemeris &amp;&amp; cd ephemeris
export JWT_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32) POSTGRES_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -hex 24)
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then open <a href="http://localhost:3005">http://localhost:3005</a> and sign in with any email
      address. There is no mail provider, so the magic link is in <code>docker compose logs ephemeris</code>.
      Read the README's <a href="{{ site.repo }}#before-you-expose-it" rel="noopener">Before you expose it</a>
      before putting it on a domain.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="readme-link">
    Full documentation lives in the project README:
    <a href="{{ site.repo }}#readme" rel="noopener">{{ site.repo }}</a>
  </p>
</section>
